{"format_version":"vbulletin-3+-thread","url":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/changeling-the-lost/10510-prologues-where-you-choose-your-seeming-in-game","id":10510,"title":"Prologues where you choose your Seeming In Game","path":["Home","Onyx Path","White Wolf Worlds","Chronicles of Darkness","Changeling: The Lost"],"posts":[{"date":"2013-11-14T09:49:25-08:00","id":10510,"title":"Prologues where you choose your Seeming In Game","content":"Okay I'm a big sucker for extended prologues, like having everyone start out as mortal for the first story or so and playing through whatever change in game. So I'm asking for everyone's ideas for methods to do this for changeling. I did this in my last game the Changeling Chronicle set in WWII where the players started in a Village in WWI and I incorporated the Wishing Well from Mysterious Places and the Mirrarae from Proverbial Monsters. The players eventually chased the boy who made a deal with the Mirrarae into the Quarry of the Wishing Well while they were hunted by a Lodge that hunted human beings. The boy made a wish to be taken away from here and the Fae living in the well took everyone in the scene to Faery. Though the players choose their Kiths out of character.<br /> <br /> Do you have any advice for ways they could choose the kiths in Characters? I was thinking when taken to Faery they could be presented with a laaarge selection of options, like choose one tool, and say if they choose the Gun they could be a Soldier Wizened, or if they choose to sweep they could be a Drudge, or if they choose the Library they could be a Librarian, Stuff like that. Any advice on expanding or elaborating the options? I figured it could also take into account how they came into faery, like if their curiosity got the better of them leading the fae to find them Darkling may be more of what they are leaning towards.<br /> <br /> <br /> The other ideal situation I want to try with my next Changeling, or really Mage as well, game would be to have the characters experience the Empty Room from Mysterious places. And on their final trip there as they lay withering from lack of food and waterthey would be presented with a path to Faery, or if a Mage game Five Paths to the Supernal.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/754-eldagusto","name":"Eldagusto","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar754_2.gif","title":"Banned","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"6435"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2013-11-14T10:18:27-08:00","id":10534,"title":"","content":"I don't like the 'choose a tool' option at all - who'd take a broom over a weapon? In an RPG? <br /> <br /> Much better idea - give them problems in Faerie and judge their approaches. Do they try to negotiate with the troll guarding the bridge, to sneak past him or to beat him into submission? Might be best to split them up for these challenges, of course, lest one player's approach dominate. Social approaches favour the Fairest (or, if they;re very deferential and favour Manipulation... Chatelaine, perhaps?), physical favour ogres and more aggressive kiths like Soldier or Hunterheart. Sneaky approaches favour Darklings, stealthier Beasts, some Wizened, maybe even Farwalkers.. The Elementals might seem tricky to fit in but with suitably weird challenges you could work that angle too","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><i>Onyx Path Freelancer: Demon Storyteller's Guide, Dark Eras, Night Horrors: Enemy Action</i><br /> <a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/demon-the-descent/825063-storyteller-s-guide-extra-material\" target=\"_blank\">Storyteller's Guide Extra Material</a><br /> <a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/demon-the-descent/899423-after-the-fall-bonus-material\" target=\"_blank\">After the Fall: Bonus Material</a>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/452-eremite","name":"Eremite","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar452_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"355"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2013-11-14T10:23:36-08:00","id":10543,"title":"","content":"Well it wasn't like you stumble on a room choose a tool, more like your keeper tells you to busy yourself what do you do grab a broom and pretend to brush? Head to library? Go in the Cellar? That type of thing. But giving them problems is an idea too, the problem with that is you pretty much direct them to a kith depending on how they react to a Problem. I mean if you are presented with a Bridge Troll what can they do to turn them into a Drudge, or a Venombite? Unless you specifically had some cobras and brooms by the bridge...","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/754-eldagusto","name":"Eldagusto","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar754_2.gif","title":"Banned","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"6435"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2013-11-14T10:29:43-08:00","id":10547,"title":"","content":"You give them multiple problems. One problem might be a True Fae's servant saying 'this room needs cleaning'. The would-be Drudge grabs a broom and helps. The Fairest convinces others, maybe event the servant who ordered them, to do the work. The ogre says 'No.' and smashes the servant's teeth in.<br /> <br /> And accept that no approach will encompass the nuances of every single kith :P Beasts are especially tricky since every kith encompasses a <i>lot</i> of different animals. <br /> <br /> The final problem might be escaping the True Fae itself. Trick it into letting you leave, you're not scoring ogre points. Injure it and run while it's distracted is, though.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><i>Onyx Path Freelancer: Demon Storyteller's Guide, Dark Eras, Night Horrors: Enemy Action</i><br /> <a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/demon-the-descent/825063-storyteller-s-guide-extra-material\" target=\"_blank\">Storyteller's Guide Extra Material</a><br /> <a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/demon-the-descent/899423-after-the-fall-bonus-material\" target=\"_blank\">After the Fall: Bonus Material</a>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/452-eremite","name":"Eremite","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar452_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"355"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2014-01-14T19:05:28-08:00","id":49459,"title":"","content":"I like the extended prologue approach and getting players to think extensively about their human selves first. I've done the symbolic choice in a game setting several times. Usually in Changeling, I have them choose their first contract that way. They start the game fresh from the Hedge and hazy, then through dreams they recover bits from their Durance, but along with that, they are also regaining their magic (I assume escape and the Hedge cost them a great deal of their magic). They have a dream about their Keeper giving them a treat for good service, and then they are offered items that represent the Universal contracts. For example, one character's Keeper was the White Lady, she offered a token of her affection: a lacy handkerchief (dream), a golden locket (hearth), a grey feather (smoke), and a silver hand mirror (mirror). Take Eremite's advise seriously-- if a item seems more practical, some might be drawn to it too much. I had a problem with one group when I associated the contracts with colors-- every assortment had a red object (hearth), black (smoke), white (dream), and silver (mirror). Well, for some reason, everyone in the group wanted the red thing-- maybe because in the mind's eye the other items seemed bland because they weren't really colors. You live and learn-- you need to make them items equally desirable and not seem to highlight one. <br /> <br /> So far, I've run games where I choose seeming and kith for the players (they agreed beforehand that it was okay). I have them choose by getting them to pick their character's favorite childhood fairytale as part of his/her character creation, then I imagine their Durance as a version of that tale in which the Keeper is the hero and the changeling is a villain or incidental. If a character like Jack and the Beanstalk, I would make him a Gargantuan Ogre or a Wood Elemental (so he would turn out to be a giant or a beanstalk).","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2002-quynce","name":"Quynce","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar2002_2.gif","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jan 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"34"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2014-01-15T10:16:04-08:00","id":49884,"title":"","content":"I have run several Changeling games that started with an &quot;extended prologue&quot; where play determines Seeming and/or Kith. One of the things I have done is use the character's Vice coupled with their Ability dot allocation to determine Seeming.<br /> <br /> <i>Lust or Gluttony/Stamina or Wits</i> - Beast<br /> <i>Greed/Manipulation or Dexterity </i>- Darkling<br /> <i>Sloth/Stamina or Resolve</i>- Elemental<br /> <i>Pride/Presence or Composure</i> - Fairest<br /> <i>Wrath or Gluttony/Strength or Resolve </i>- Ogre<br /> <i>Envy/Intelligence or Wits </i>- Wizened<br /> <br /> I used this method for Seeming, because you could usually tell what kind of character a person wants to play by just looking at these two aspects of their stats. Also, by taking agency away from the players in this regard really touched on an important aspect of the game for me. The Seeming is what <b>happened</b> to you as the result of your Durance. It is a collection of scars that will never fade and the survivor must learn to live with and accept.<br /> <br /> After this step, myself and the players roleplay out their abduction through the Hedge and into the Fairest of Lands. I describe how the Thorns tear at their flesh and their mortality, flensing away the dross of their bodies so that by the time that their Keeper has brought them into Arcadia, all that really remains are the purest aspects of their &quot;soul&quot; and identity. It is at this stage that the fairy changes the person into, what they feel is, the outward reflection of the reason they took them in the first place. I let the players then pick a Kith of their choosing, and I ask them to pick one of their Skills and use it as a metaphysical justification of the kith. This linked Skill is then used throughout the &quot;prologue&quot; in a series of extended rolls which determine the effectiveness of their escape from Arcadia and also to determine how many bonus points the character can then use to purchase Changeling specific merits.<br /> <br />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1996-firefly-night","name":"Firefly Night","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1996_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jan 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"150"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2014-01-15T13:34:23-08:00","id":50034,"title":"","content":"Oooh, I really like that approach, Firefly Night! I completely agree that the Durance is an abduction-- a wresting away of control and identity more complete than can readily be imagined. I like to choose their Seeming and Kith to reproduce that loss. To some it might seem extreme to take over that aspect of the character, but playing through the shock of reconciling the life Before Durance with the life After Durance is where much of the interesting game play is. I try to combine their original selves with the myth or story and give it a solid twist toward the dark side of fairy tales. I have to say, though, I find the &quot;combine vice and ability&quot; idea something to seriously consider for future games-- it seems like a great indicator of the direction of the character.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2002-quynce","name":"Quynce","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar2002_2.gif","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jan 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"34"}]},"thankedby":[]}]}